 -- Lorenzo "Palinuro" Faletra <palinuro@parrotsec.org>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 23:35:39 +0200

opencode (2.0.18-0parrot1) echo; urgency=medium

  * New upstream version 2.0.18.
  * Harden the build against silent stalls: run the heartbeat as
    debian/heartbeat.sh (a plain shell script, directly testable, no
    make-recipe quoting hazards) and extend it into a stall detector:
    it tracks the kernel oom_kill counter and, after two consecutive
    samples without any runnable (R-state) build process, dumps each
    build process's wait site (kernel wait channel, syscall, RSS) so a
    hung vite/esbuild/bun step leaves a diagnosable log instead of an
    empty one. The previous arm64 builds sat with load 0.00 and all
    build processes idle for ~50 minutes until the inactivity
    watchdog killed them; the detector will show whether that was a
    userspace deadlock (futex wait), a dead worker after an OOM kill
    (pipe wait + oom_kill increment) or a merely slow CPU-bound
    build (R-state processes at 100% cpu).
  * Bound the build's network steps: the models.dev catalog refresh
    now runs under timeout(1) (300s) so a hung fetch cannot stall the
    build, and the Bun toolchain download uses curl stall detection
    (--speed-limit 1024 --speed-time 60, retries) instead of running
    unbounded.
  * Make the OpenCode Web desktop launcher fully automatic: add an
    opencode-web wrapper command that starts `opencode serve`, prints
    the complete login (the username, hardcoded upstream as "opencode",
    together with the generated session password) and opens the web UI
    in the default browser with xdg-open, instead of leaving the user
    to copy-paste the printed URL by hand. The plain serve output only
    shows the password without the username. xdg-utils moves back from
    Suggests to Depends: the automatic browser launch is part of the
    launcher's function on amd64 (unused but harmless on arm64, where
    the launcher is not installed since the arm64 binaries omit the
    web UI).
  * Skip the embedded web UI on arm64: the web UI is produced by a large
    vite build whose arm64 builds have repeatedly stalled silently on
    the build workers (see the stall detector above). Pass --skip-web-ui
    on arm64 and do not install the OpenCode Web desktop launcher there;
    the binary embeds an empty app archive while the terminal UI, the
    CLI and the headless `serve` API server remain fully functional.
    Revisit once the stall detector has identified the arm64 vite stall.

 -- Lorenzo "Palinuro" Faletra <palinuro@parrotsec.org>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 15:28:22 +0200

opencode (2.0.5-0parrot1) echo; urgency=medium

  * New upstream version 2.0.5.
  * Fix the build heartbeat's memory reading: the awk program inside
    the heartbeat echo contained backslash-escaped double quotes, which
    the buildd shell passes through literally inside "$( ... )" quoting,
    so mawk rejected the program ("unexpected character") and every
    heartbeat reported an empty memory value. Rewrite the awk program
    quote-free (print int($2/1024) with the MB suffix in the echo text
    instead) and add a snapshot of the build processes (state, %cpu,
    command) to every heartbeat: the arm64 build's heartbeats
    consistently reported load 0.00, which suggests the build sits
    blocked rather than merely slow, and the process snapshot will tell
    CPU-bound builds from blocked ones. Verified by running the exact
    recipe bytes through make with /bin/sh (dash) and mawk, like the
    buildd does.
  * Refresh the bundled models.dev provider catalog snapshot
    (packages/core/src/models-dev/snapshot.txt) at build time with
    upstream's own update-models-snapshot.ts: the snapshot committed in
    the release tarball lagged the live catalog by 35 providers and
    1184 models, and the binary only refreshes it live at runtime, so
    offline and first-run users started from a stale model list. The
    refresh is best-effort (a failed fetch keeps the upstream snapshot
    and prints a warning) and honours the OPENCODE_MODELS_URL override.
    The refreshed snapshot is a build artifact: debian/source/options
    keeps it out of regenerated source diffs.

 -- Lorenzo "Palinuro" Faletra <palinuro@parrotsec.org>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 01:03:26 +0200

